\pard\plain \ltrpar\qc \li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11304688 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs22\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \f31506\fs22\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs110 \ltrch\fcs0 \f168\fs110\insrsid11304688\charrsid16206365 H}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs96 \ltrch\fcs0 \f168\fs96\insrsid11304688\charrsid16206365 eart}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs110 \ltrch\fcs0 \f168\fs110\insrsid11304688\charrsid16206365 T}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs96 \ltrch\fcs0 \f168\fs96\insrsid11304688\charrsid16206365 heft
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11304688 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid11304688 \tab 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs32\insrsid11304688 \tab Good Friend, allow me to tell you a parable, a tale God desires we learn from. It is the story of the First Canine. A man named Ciraberos.
\par \tab Ciraberos was the first creation of the Lord capable- not of simply looking and interacting with the Kingdom He had made- but understanding it. In this understanding we mean to say, he could perceive his place as not simply alive, but as }{
\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f171\fs32\insrsid11304688\charrsid14759633 being}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs32\insrsid11304688 
. And this set him apart from the Ferals, the lesser beasts, who lived only to satisfy the needs of their life moment to moment. 
Ciraberos could make some sense of the passing of time, could come to have expectations for the future, not simply days but years ahead. He could plan for that future. He could look back retrospectively on the time that had passed\'85 and regret.
\par \tab To this end
 the Creator, the one we now know as God, gave Ciraberos three heads. He knew, in His wisdom, that had he but one, his thoughts would be consumed by lamentation and regret for the past, trepidation and fear for the future. For even in the good world our C
reator had given him, there was pain. The perfect, flawless, unmarred world He had made was despoiled by the want and choices of his creations, from the moment they set foot upon it. 
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi720\li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11304688 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs32\insrsid11304688 
But Ciraberos was His chosen creation, as close to his own likeness as the Creator had ever made. And He was merciful, and thus wished for Ciraberos to know a good life, a content life. As painless a life as possible. 
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11304688 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs32\insrsid11304688 \tab 
And so, Ciraberos was endowed with these three heads- one that always looked back, and watched his tail. One that always looked ahead, to the horizon and to the future. And one that stayed firmly rooted in the}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f171\fs32\insrsid3821747  present}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs32\insrsid11304688 , to focus on the tasks at hand and the joy that was to be had in the moment. Each head a different perspective }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f171\fs32\insrsid3821747 to see the world from, each an assigned role}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs32\insrsid11304688 .
\par \tab But Ciraberos came to resent this choice}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs32\insrsid3821747 , }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs32\insrsid11304688 and }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs32\insrsid3821747 
in doing so, resented }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs32\insrsid11304688 his God\rquote 
s mercy. For in creating him thusly, free to claim this Kingdom under his own will, free to want, to hunger, to yearn for more, the Creator had unavoidably created in him the sin of Desire. Ciraberos desired, not just what he had been given already but }{
\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f171\fs32\insrsid11304688\charrsid7695183 more}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs32\insrsid11304688 . Forever on, this desire burned in him, an unquenchable flame\'85 and it began to drive him mad.
\par \tab Ciraberos was immortal,}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs32\insrsid3821747  not immune to death, but}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs32\insrsid11304688  immune to the ravages o
f time, and the more time he lived, the more he desired to change in the past. The more he desired to lay claim to in the future. His central head, grounded to the present, remained the voice of reason. Focused, as ever, on what could be done, what could 
be accomplished, }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f171\fs32\insrsid11304688\charrsid8602100 then}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs32\insrsid11304688 . Moment to moment.
\par \tab But in time the other two became too loud. Far too loud\'85 and too sinful. And no resolution could be found, no middle-ground met. He was a being of extremes.
\par \tab Ciraberos became wrathful, gluttonous, depressive, greedy, and most of all, ruled by his }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f171\fs32\insrsid11304688\charrsid8602100 desires}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs32\insrsid11304688 
. And in his sinful state, he consumed and destroyed much of the Kingdom he\rquote d been meant to shepherd. His armies of feral dyre overtook, devoured and conquered all they could reach. They burned, and killed, and raped,
 until their Kingdom was desolate and all of the innocent had fled. Leaving only the ravenous.
\par \tab In the end, it was Ciraberos\rquote  own dyre soldiers who slew him. }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs32\insrsid3821747 W}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs32\insrsid11304688 
hile his flesh could not age, it was no more immune to violence than the feral dyre. In their ravenous hunger, they piled upon him like logs heaped on a pyre. And with him, the First World died.
\par \tab For time untold, there was nothing more. The Creator mourned what Ciraberos had done to the Kingdom he had gifted him with, and held his soul in Judgment- }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs32\insrsid3821747 the first world}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs32\insrsid11304688  }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs32\insrsid3821747 he }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs32\insrsid11304688 had created,}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f171\fs32\insrsid3821747  abandoned long ago, for it}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs32\insrsid11304688  was imperfect, fractured and poisonous. When darkness overtakes the moon, we have a glimpse }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f171\fs32\insrsid3821747 briefly through a doorway into}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs32\insrsid11304688  this place. This realm is where He banished his failures to, and it became Ciraberos\rquote 
 new Kingdom. A place he would know only regret, only pain, only loss. We common folk now call this place Hell.
\par \tab Long following Ciraberos\rquote  fall, our Creator at last perfected His work. And this we know, is when He brought life to Canus. The first, most perfect Canine. Our Patriarch.
\par \tab Canus had but one head, but was still capable of retrospective, of looking ahead, and of living in the moment. He would need to learn to balance these perspectives in one mind, and his struggle continues on in us, to t
his day. Of course, with this understanding of the world, came with it again the sin of Desire. So, to temper this, the Creator reduced his time. No longer would his flesh be immortal, but bound to the same laws of entropy, the cycle of life and death, th
at the dyre and the ferals, that all of the lesser entities, flora and fauna alike, were bound to. Only his soul would remain immortal.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi720\li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11304688 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs32\insrsid11304688 
In this he was unlike the Creator. But while Ciraberos squandered eternity, Canus would treasure his}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs32\insrsid3821747  shorter}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs32\insrsid11304688  time. He wou
ld live each life more firmly rooted in the present, in the tasks laid out before him that need be done, and in the joy of each passing moment. He would learn from the past, and desire to better his future, but when one short lifetime ended\'85
 he would forget. And he would begin anew, again.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11304688 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs32\insrsid11304688 \tab 
The cycle of death and rebirth. The greatest gift He ever gave us is a punishment for our ancestors\rquote  transgressions. This in and of itself is a lesson, Good Friend. Punishment, and moreover\'85 death\'85 can be a gift.
\par \tab The Cr
eator made many of these Patriarchs, and Mothers. Canus was merely the first. In time he was given a wife, and many followers. These early canines were the First Wolves. The first souls that began the cycle. Like the moon waxes and wanes, so do our lives,
 
brightening and dimming, and throughout them we know regret, and hope. But now, we must turn to look to the past. We must lift our heads to look to the future. And for either we can only imagine, remember. Our eyes can only see the present. The sky above,
 the dirt at our feet. The Kingdom He has given us. 
\par \tab Our memory is imperfect. Time wears it away. And }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f171\fs32\insrsid11304688\charrsid9443921 no}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs32\insrsid11304688 
 man can recall their previous lives. This is His plan. This is meant to protect us from the sin that knowing so much can bring. It is a mercy.
\par \tab But the
 Sin of Desire is still within us. One can liken it to a poisonous seed which can take root at any time. To want is natural. To desire is dangerous and less easy to temper. It drives men to take leave of their senses, of their morality and rationality. Le
ft unchecked, even in the short lifespans we now live, it can lead us to burn down the world. To echo the mistakes of Ciraberos.
\par \tab The souls who commit such foul acts are claimed for Judgment. They are marked and removed from God\rquote s good plan, from the cycle of life and death, and sent to Ciraberos\rquote 
 realm. Hell is a dark and fractured place, Good Friends, hell is a barren land of pain and regret, and little else. 
\par \tab And so, we must live these short lives, thankful for whatever little we are given, and fight against this desire for more. For it will destroy us, if we let it fester. It will consume us, as it did Ciraberos.
\par \tab Look, Good Friend, at the earth beneath your feet. At the sky above your head. Even if all you can see of it is through the bars of a cell. Ev
en if the land is barren, and yields nothing for you to eat. This is the Kingdom we have been given, and we must make the most of what we have, and be grateful for it.
\par \tab Because Hell awaits. And no quenching of desire is worth forsaking the good world He gave us. No sin in this one short life is worth suffering for eternity.
\par \tab }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs32\insrsid11304688\charrsid14759633 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid11304688 
\par 
\par \tab Chapter 1 \endash  First Meeting, The Hudson Estate
\par 
\par \tab I consider myself a man possessing of diligent politeness and social tact, and careful consideration of propriety in most circumstances. So, it was a point of shame that on this day, in my harried and panicked state, I forgot myself.
\par \tab I had little trouble shouldering through the throngs of tailored shoulders, cravats, laced necklines, and all manner of finery adorning the unsuspecting and innocent partygoers in the Hudson\rquote 
s Great Hall. The Hudsons were a family of stout, fine-cheek-boned Bull Terriers, exqu
isite in their Pedigree, but far smaller in stature than I.  And though they had many a guest at this gathering, I had a head and shoulders on most, regardless. Which made it far too easy to push past and through the intimate }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 
\ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid21193 crowd}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid11304688  in my rush to the Solarium.
\par \tab 
I heard no one call out for me, save proclamations of offense, and not a soul followed when I burst through the Solarium door, the glass rattling in its ornate iron binding behind me. That my sudden departure from the party was not worrisome to them w
as hardly surprising- I was in no one\rquote s confidence, in this estate. I hadn\rquote t even been on the guest list. I was a late appointment, an interloper, signed off on by the Hudson family\rquote s personal attending Templar. And he wasn\rquote 
t even present; he was in Absolution with Father Helstrom during the whole duration of my weeklong stay on these grounds. The Hudson family felt that only one Templar on the grounds was what was proper.
\par \tab It seems to me that no righteous man who is secure in his place in God\rquote s heart shoul
d object to the presence of a holy minder. And what difference could two make as opposed to one? But, as my Bishop had reminded me upon taking up this task, Pedigree Lords can cling stubbornly to mortal tradition, regardless of whether we feel it serves G
od\rquote s ends. So long as they maintain the holy doctrine in their lives, it is not our place to question any bureaucratic quibbles. The Priests and Bishops handle that.
\par \tab I wished- as I stumbled out into mottled sunlight, blinking against the glare filtering down through the small citrus trees in this stiflingly hot glass cage- that Templar Olvar was here. While he wasn\rquote 
t from my Parish and had taken a different path in his clerical studies to attain his ordination, he }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f171\fs36\insrsid11304688\charrsid15613186 did}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid11304688 
 know of my troubles. He had been a frequent visitor at our Solstice Mass in Auldfuster, and I knew he\rquote d been present at least once when the subject of my dietary weakness came up in conversation.
\par \tab He would be able to do little, of course. But it would have been comforting to have an elder Brother at my side in my time of suffering.
\par \tab The thickening, choking sensation in my throat was growing in intensity, with each passing moment. I knew what came next. I came to a stop in the center of the Solarium, an intricate, multicolored brick spiral in the
 shape of a waxing moon, held in the clutch of a bed of fragrant hibiscus bushes and lemon trees. I looked, frantically, for anywhere that I might rest my body\'85
 and came away with nothing. The Lady of the house may have had a liking for citrus and the luxu
ry to take her morning walks in a warm, sunlit, miniature forest, but she clearly did not prefer to take her leisure here. There were no stone benches; no chairs of any kind.
\par \tab I could lie on the ground out here, and I probably wouldn\rquote t be seen by the elites inside. The thought lingered in my desperate mind for longer than it should have, as the first labored gasps left my panting muzzle\'85.
\par \tab Ridiculous. The indecency of it, the }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f171\fs36\insrsid11304688\charrsid6451099 weakness}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid11304688 
 it would portray! I had only received my investiture as an Acolyte of the
 Templar Order last spring. This was but my third posting at a Pedigree house. I was not seeking a permanent appointment, given the nature of my ordination, but that hardly mattered. We represented the whole of the Order when we formally took up a positio
n in a Manor home, or any posting. I was not going to embarrass my Parish. I }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f171\fs36\insrsid11304688\charrsid3351268 was}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f171\fs36\insrsid11304688  not}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid11304688 .
\par \tab So then, where could I lay myself down for a time? Wait for this to pass\'85. 
\par \tab My rasping breaths filled my skull, joined now by the high-pitched whistle that signaled that very soon, I would start choking. I\rquote d only had a bite of the little fried cake; I hadn\rquote 
t eaten enough that I earnestly thought my life might be in danger.
\par \tab Lord help me, when would I learn? Especially in gathers like this, when pungent alcohol was flowing
, when all the Ladies and Gentlemen in attendance were perfumed and powdered, when there was so much fatty meat and savory desserts spread out in the serving hall\'85 I had to smell my food before I ate it! If I\rquote 
d taken even a cursory sniff of the little fried cakes on that platter, I would have smelled the crab in them.
\par \tab I\rquote d realized it the second I\rquote d swallowed a bite. Shellfish had been my bane since childhood, but that didn\rquote t mean I hadn\rquote t enough experiences to know the taste by now. And so began my flight from the hall.
\par \tab If I\rquote d been calm and reasoned about this, I would have headed for a parlor. A spare bedroom. Anywhere I might find privacy that wasn\rquote t a cobblestone-inlaid Solarium with no places of rest in sight.
\par \tab God, where could I go? How did I weather this without bringing disgrace to His name, and my Parish?
\par \tab I had not heard the Solarium door open again behind me, but I was deep into the grove of miniature trees, and thoroughly distracted enough that it was no surprise.  All the same, I nearly leapt out of my spats when a gentle, quiet man\rquote 
s voice spoke from behind me, along the pathway back to the Great Hall.
\par \tab \'93Are you alright, Lord?\'94
\par \tab I spun on my right footpad to discover who had caught me in my moment of struggle. The sight that greeted me was not any soul I would have expected\'85
 nor anyone I had taken note of in the manor prior to this. He was feline, black-furred, not diminutive but not tall either, save for the prominent ears that adorned his forehead. He was clearly not gentry, which almost went w
ithout saying in the heart of Amuresca- he was, as I said, feline after all.
\par \tab He wore a simple but clean white servant\rquote s blouse, well-worn but also well-tailored britches, cotton spats and a common enough accoutrement amongst kitchen and labor staff- a leather-braced men\rquote 
s corset. It accented his already lean figure and unfortunately added to his diminutive-ness. But he looked like some of the felines I\rquote d seen that worked at the grounds\rquote  bakery, and if you were going to be lifting sacks of flour all day, ba
ck support was all but essential.
\par \tab Lord, how my mind wandered when I was deprived of air.
\par \tab What was important here wasn\rquote t the man\rquote s corset, or how he wore it\'85 it\rquote s that I\rquote d had the good fortune to be found not by one of the Pedigree Lords, but by a worker 
who would know the grounds, and could help me. And if I embarrassed myself with the help, it was far less injurious than it would have been to lose face with the family here.
\par \tab I gagged on my own saliva and swallowed as well as I could a few times, trying to get the words, \'93Not\'85 a Lord,\'94 out. I don\rquote t know if I really succeeded.
\par \tab \'93Ah, yes. You\rquote re the House Templar, right?\'94 he asked in that respectful, but vaguely guarded tone most servants used when conversing with me. As a matter of course, anyone in a household I was overseeing would be wary of my presence.

\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi720\li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11304688 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid11304688 
I could answer that easily enough with a nod, so I did. He inclined his muzzle up and I think he may have tried to give a polite smile, but it was lost in all the worry. \'93You\'85 probably should have saved y
our breath, there. Your station matters less than your health, after all. How can I\'85 I wish to help\'85\'94
\par I blinked through a narrowing field of vision, my eyelids having grown heavy and swollen. If I didn\rquote t find somewhere to tough this out soon enough, I\rquote d lose the ability to navigate the grounds altogether.
\par I opened my muzzle to ask him where I could go, and instead gave a wracking cough. His already oversized green-gold eyes widened, and he took a tentative step towards me, reaching out a hand while still keeping a respectable distance. \'93Are you choking?
\'94 he asked. 
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11304688 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid11304688 \tab 
I shook my head in between a few wheezing coughs, sucking as much breath into my deprived lungs as I could, before saying with a weak, thready voice, \'93Not\'85 choking. Need\'85 somewhere\'85 to lie down\'85\'94 
\par \tab \'93I understand,\'94 he said, and without further pause, with a confidence and immediacy of action that you didn\rquote 
t usually see in a servant, took one of my hands in his and began to lead me down one fork of the Solarium path, towards I knew-not-where.
\par \tab For some reason as he led me along- perhaps because my mind got fuzzy around the edges and tended to wander when my 
breath was being stolen from me- I found myself fixated on his slender, dark fingers clasping my own thick, clumsy hand. Our furs were opposing colors, so it was very easy to pick out the margins. His paws were soft\'85 softer than a servant\rquote 
s had any right to be. Perhaps he didn\rquote t work in the bakery after all.
\par \tab They weren\rquote t extended at the moment, but I could also tell his claws were manicured. Like a Pedigree Lady, or a dandy might. Some Lords preferred it as a sign of their domesticity, and some workers\rquote  pr
ofessions required it. Nurses and those who worked with delicate fabrics, for instance. I wondered which he was.
\par \tab My own claws were as thick and cumbersome as the rest of me. And dirty\'85 oh, Lord, how humiliating. I\rquote d forgotten to scrub them after training this morning. Father Helstrom would have had my head if he knew.
\par \tab He pushed open a glass door ahead of us, much like the one I had come through, but this one leading outside to the courtyard abutting the deer park. We were at the back of the manor; I knew at least that much from my brief time here.
\par \tab \'93Where\'85?\'94 I choked out.
\par \tab \'93The Dovecote,\'94 he explained, hurrying us across the grounds towards a corner of the courtyard I\rquote d not had much occasion to traverse. It was less planted than the areas closer to the hou
se, save the ever-present rose bushes and the occasional well-manicured tree. 
\par \tab I had no ability to question his logic, but internally, I had to assume I\rquote d been misunderstood. The Dovecote at this estate was very large, but it was still an oversized stone 
birdhouse, essentially. There was unlikely to be anywhere inside to sit, let alone lie down, and if I did, I would be covered in bird excrement. 
\par \tab I soon discovered his plan when we rounded the rose bushes, though. Outside the Dovecote, tucked back amongst the cherry trees and a fresh down blanket of fallen blossoms from the early spring we\rquote 
d been treated to, was an elegant and very wide stone bench. Somewhere even a man of my bulk would be able to rest his body.
\par \tab By now I was nearly gagging on my tongue, so the feline lad had to help me sit down. I could do little other than disentangle our fingers, and give his hand a soft pat, to show my gratitude.
\par \tab He sat down gingerly beside me, looking on like he wanted to do more. But I knew}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid14890194 -}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid11304688  although I could not expl
ain to him as much}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid14890194 -}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid11304688  that there was little more anyone could do to help me, at this point. I simply had to wait for it to pass.

\par \tab I felt his eyes on mine as I slowly stretched my body out, denying myself the useless gesture of scratching or pawing at my throat. It wouldn\rquote t help. I knitted my fingers over my chest, to keep myself from doing it.
\par \tab \'93Some kind of affliction of your body?\'94 he guessed, softly.
\par \tab Again, I nodded. I could do that much, at least.
\par \tab He was silent a spell, before murmuring, \'93How unfortunate. I\rquote m sorry. It will pass?\'94
\par \tab I nodded.
\par \tab \'93Not quickly, though.\'94
\par \tab This time, I shook my head.
\par \tab I felt him fidget from beside me, just a slight tensing, and a flick of his small tail. I could sense his discomfort, and of course wished I was not burdening others. Even someone as lowly as house staff. 
\par \tab My head was close to where he was sat, my legs propped up on the arm of the right-hand side of the bench. I had my neck craned back, trying to find a position of comfort that kept my throat as open as possible. 
But when I turned my head to the side, I found my nose inches away from where his hand paw was clutching at his thin knee. And I noticed something I had not, before.
\par \tab I didn\rquote t know what variety of feline he was, nor did it matter. Huudari species were comm
on enough in our Empire, especially amongst the worker classes. But whatever his kin, there were the subtlest hints of spotted patterns beneath the black of his fur. An equatorial cat, then. Someone from closer to the tropics. The black variation in his f
ur was a deviation from God\rquote 
s plan for his people, and an unfortunate one, at that. Not only would it avail him poorly in a hotter climate, it was a dreadfully common coloration, for a species whose limited assets usually included their exotic beauty.
\par \tab My target in this investigation, the whole reason I was here, was thusly blessed. She\rquote d yet to show herself, either on these grounds or in the surrounding township, but if she did, she\rquote 
d be hard to miss. She was a feline of renowned beauty, with a golden, intricately-patterned foreign coat. Her charm had availed her thus-far, seducing and fooling many a dull-witted, lascivious gentleman. }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f171\fs36\insrsid5124764 S}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid11304688 he\rquote d have no such power over me.
\par \tab The beauty of this place of respite began to occur to me as I lay there considering my unf
ortunate situation, as well as the misfortune of the kind fellow beside me. My vision as I looked upwards was of a blue, midday sky, patchy with wisps of clouds, and the fluttering hints of pink from the remaining blooms caught in the cherry tree\rquote 
s branche
s. I could feel that I was lying on a thin bed of the fallen blossoms; no one had cleaned this bench in some time. The distant fluttering and cooing of the denizens of the Dovecote was the only sound save the mild wind, and the overwhelming floral scent d
rowned out any unpleasant odors I may have smelled from the birds otherwise.
\par \tab It was almost romantic. Not in an amorous sense, but in the way poets and writers might characterize the word. I could all but hear my Amurescan instructor, exasperated- \lquote }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid9661027 there ar
e many definitions of the word}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid6776803 , such as\'85 }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid11304688 suggesting a view of reality that is idealized\rquote 
. I had asked him about it once when he had patiently explained to me that not all \lquote romantic\rquote  poets were writing of mortal love, but rather of a \lquote romantic\rquote  view of the 
world. It was one of those odd memories that had formed so clearly in my mind, I would never forget it. 
\par \tab With my state of rest, and not least of all the admittedly quiet beauty of this place, came a relaxation in my chest. Slowly, I was able to steady my 
shallow breaths, fight back the panic, and fall into my meditations. I would weather this. I always had.
\par \tab \'93You seem to be improving,\'94 the fellow beside me noted after a time. \'93Are you\'85 out of the woods? I am not certain how long the danger in this lasts for you. But I would rather not leave you if this could yet take a turn for the worse.\'94

\par \tab \'93No,\'94 I croaked. \'93Not\'85 out of danger\'85 yet.\'94
\par \tab I didn\rquote t want to ask him to stay, but there really was a chance this could still get worse before it got better. And I had no one else.
\par \tab \'93Then I\rquote ll stay at your side,\'94 he said, calmly. I felt him shift, and then a slim shadow as he leaned over me. His eyes briefly looked down into my own before moving over my chest. \'93Shouldn\rquote t we take the\'85 that\rquote 
s some kind of a weapon harness, right? Shouldn\rquote t we take that off? Isn\rquote t it restricting your chest?\'94
\par \tab He was right. I felt like a fool for not realizing it earlier. It\rquote s the first thing I should have done.
\par \tab I gave a tight nod 
and shifted up, grimacing around a short gasp. Before I could prop myself up further on my elbow, I felt his thigh and knee slide under my back, holding me up just enough. He reached around and beneath my cloak with deft hands, and began navigating the st
raps to undo my chest harness. It was, as he\rquote d said, a weapon belt. But it was also something of a badge of office; the family here found it far too intimidating for me to be armed inside the house. I had no blades on my person }{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid6060658 right now}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid11304688 , so there was no issue with a civilian handling it.
\par \tab I couldn\rquote t tell him how to remove the thing, and it could be complicated if you didn\rquote t wear one yourself }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid1594290 regularly}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f171\fs36\insrsid11304688 , but he managed it after a few moments of figuring out the strap and }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid6060658 buckle and}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid11304688  slid one end 
\lquote round my back to the other side of my chest, before tugging it free of my torso. He draped it over the side of the bench, I think, then set to un-bunching my cloak from where I\rquote 
d lain down atop it, smoothing it out down my sides to my belt line, clearly intending to make me more comfortable. He showed not a single sign of discomfort or awkwardness while doing so, either. Probably a tailor, then. I suppose that made sense.
\par \tab He stood afterwards, and smiled down at me in a way that was almost so delicate as to be un-masculine. I felt a lightness in my chest that was unbefitting the allergic reaction I was suffering, and wondered at myself for a moment.
\par \tab \'93Better?\'94 he asked.
\par \tab I nodded, clearing my throat as well as I could. \'93Much,\'94 I agreed. And it was. I\rquote d ingested very little of what was causing my plight}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid7406643 , }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f171\fs36\insrsid11304688 but the panic and the harness may have exacerbated my reaction. I was beginning to calm, some of the pressure was off my chest. And\'85 someone here had seen fit to show me kindness.
\par \tab I would thank God in my}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid926357  evening}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid11304688  prayers}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid926357 , b}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid11304688 ut for now, I sent my praise heavenward through my thoughts. This could have been far worse.
\par \tab \'93Rest here,\'94 the servant said, settling a hand once more on mine for a moment, giving it just the slightest squeeze, before slipping away. \'93If you\rquote re going to be alright now, I must return to my duties.\'94
\par \tab I was feeling better\'85 far better. So, I nodded. And I tried to smile, to assure him. I knew from experience my features were puffy and ugly though, so I\rquote m certain I was a fright.
\par \tab He took it in stride, giving me one last, quiet, \'93Take care, young Templar,\'94 before turning and heading back towards the manor, the way we had come.
\par \tab I\rquote m not sure how long I lay there. It may have been an hour. I\rquote m certain at least it was not two. I watched the cherry blossoms fall}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid9395373 , }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f171\fs36\insrsid11304688 listened to the doves sing, and I thanked the Lord for every blessing that lightened these moments of pain. Truly, I had been given the gift of grace this day. And I\rquote d not soon forget it.
\par \tab Strangely, it wasn\rquote t the feline servant who came to check on me again. It was a woman, a spotted pig whom I\rquote 
d seen serving in the Great Hall earlier. She came with fresh, cold water from the well for me, treating me like I were one of her piglets, like a fussy mother worried after her 
young. She helped me to my feet after insisting I drink the whole of the pewter cup she\rquote 
d brought out to me, and upon the fifth or sixth time I assured her I could find my way back to my quarters on my own, she fearlessly batted me on the arm and said, \'93You young lads! All of you, a-feared to admit when you\rquote 
re under the weather. On your way, then. I\rquote ll have oatmeal and plain bread sent up for your dinner, later.\'94
\par \tab She, at least, knew of my dietary failings. The whole kitchen staff did. But it wasn\rquote t their fault that I\rquote d eaten a crab cake without knowing it\'85 the food at the party wasn\rquote t catered for me, after all. And I\rquote 
d been careless.
\par \tab \'93Oh, ma\rquote am,\'94 I said, clutching at my throat even though I shouldn\rquote t have been. It was so hard to avoid. I spoke slowly, between breaths. \'93The\'85 feline fellow\'85 who told you\'85 where to find me. Where does\'85
 he work? Want to\'85 thank him.\'94
\par \tab She twisted up her snout oddly, lifting a brow. \'93One of the serving lasses brought word from the Main Hall you was out here, in need of tendin\rquote . I don\rquote 
t know who told her. Feline? We have no felines on staff, none that are male, anyway. And I\rquote d know.\'94 She leaned in, like what she was about to say was conspiratorial. \'93The lady of the house thinks they }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f171\fs36\insrsid11304688\charrsid4203937 reek}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid11304688 , man-cats. And I don\rquote t entirely disagree.\'94
\par \tab I didn\rquote t know what to make of what she\rquote d said. \'93Then\'85 he works the grounds, maybe?\'94
\par \tab The pig shook her head. \'93No, lad. None I know of. Not on the whole of the Estate.\'94
\par \tab Something, I don\rquote t know what, perhaps a flash of insight from above, made me remember the gentle touch as his hands smoothed down my cloak. Under my back, along my torso\'85 my belt\'85.
\par \tab My hand shot down to my metal belt hook, where I kept my-
\par \tab Nothing.
\par \tab I was in no condition to run. I was in }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f171\fs36\insrsid11304688\charrsid4203937 no condition to run}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid11304688 , but I did, leaving the pig aghast
 and yelling her outrage at my foolishness to my back. I rushed in through the gardener\rquote s entrance, navigating my way into the servant\rquote s hallways, headed for the one staircase that connected these hidden corridors- the places in this house I
\rquote d memorized ahead of this }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f171\fs36\insrsid11304688\charrsid923775 so}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid11304688  carefully, because I\rquote 
d been so very certain the thief would need to use these to pilfer the artifact- to the second story.
\par \tab My lungs and my throat nearly instantly closed to a clench again, and everything was burning and spinning, my field of vision shrinking and sporadically bursting in black dots as I tore down the hallway.
\par \tab Not to the Lord\rquote s quarters. To the humbler rooms that were in the west wing. The Templar\rquote s Residence. My room, while I stayed here.
\par \tab The room I had insisted we keep the Artifact hidden away in. Because I had changed the locks, because the thief would never expect it was here, because }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f171\fs36\insrsid11304688\charrsid923775 I would know}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid11304688  when a feline fitting that description made an appearance and }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f171\fs36\insrsid11304688\charrsid923775 we needed to catch her in the act-}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f171\fs36\insrsid11304688 
\par \tab }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid11304688 My door was open.
\par \tab I came to a halt, falling to my knees, barely able to draw breath. And I checked my belt again, to be sure. I don\rquote t know why. I had closed and locked my door when I\rquote d left this morning.
\par \tab My door was open.
\par \tab And my keys were gone.
\par \tab Stumbling to my feet, clutching at the }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid11618594 doorknob}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid11304688 , I staggered just inside the }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f171\fs36\insrsid11618594 room}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f171\fs36\insrsid11304688 , knowing what I\rquote d see. Namely, the open chest in the corner.
\par \tab And so it was.
\par \tab What I hadn\rquote t expected\'85 was the small, folded letter on my desk.
