\par \tab I was seventeen when I first heard of her. The Heart Thief.
\par \tab I was in the Choir, assisting Brother Dillard and Brother Adam}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639 
 in the twice-yearly painstaking task of scrubbing the grout. It was a job my diligence was well-suited to, and the other lay monks were happy to have the help each 
year. I found it a relaxing task, unlike most. Satisfying. Making pure and pristine what had previously been dirtied and sullied.
\par \tab If only life were so simple.
\par \tab Back then, we didn\rquote t realize the thief was a woman. The series of burglaries- some of which had
 reportedly ended violently, even up to and including the death of a housekeeper, and eventually two from our very own Order- had led to the obvious assumption that the thief would be male. And indeed, when I heard the Brothers speaking on the subject of 
the theft, they knew little more. A feline man. Foreign, most likely, although we\rquote 
d since learned- and I could confirm this personally now- that they were either born and raised in Amuresca, or had voice-trained to have no hint of any foreign accent. So, someone of foreign ancestry who\rquote 
d been born here, most likely. Or a young transplant.
\par \tab The Templar Order had very little information on the feline thief until they\rquote d come for one of our own relics. Investigating burglaries- even from estates and private coll
ections- was hardly our mission, after all. Unless the items stolen had been taken out from under a Templar\rquote 
s own nose, which had happened in a few of the cases but was hardly the norm, we were unlikely to even hear of it. Let alone concern ourselves with it. Our priority was the health of our Pedigree and our Nation\rquote 
s souls, and the protection and sanctity of the bond between the Church, and the powerful elites who ruled the Country. Our people were stronger when both arms of Amuresca were linked hand in hand, thusly. 
\par \tab Meddling unnecessarily in one another\rquote s affairs could tip that balance, foster resentment and make it harder for both of us to fulfill our roles within society. So for some time, how long I cannot say, the Heart Thief\rquote 
s crimes went beneath our notice. Local law in the many different regions she\rquote 
d struck in may have known of her, or at least known a skilled thief had made a target of them, and Pedigree families had begun to gossip about the bold thefts and trade stories of the man they imagined would be responsible\'85
 but all of this may have remained as it was, in disconnected accounts, if not for the crime she eventually committed.
\par \tab The Heart of Faith was one of the most well-known relics of our Church, and had been the final stop in a holy pil
grimage that had spanned over a thousand years, culminating each cycle on the solstice. In all that time, it had never been moved from the humble Abbey on Mount D\rquote 
Amur, a mountain that was in truth little more than a large hill in Amurfolk. The grounds surrounding it, and most of the adjoining townships, were Holy Land, the First Parish. We\rquote 
d gone once as children. I was young at the time, but I remembered walking through the ancient hanging gardens with Nicholas. We weren\rquote t permitted to enter the Sacristy to pray before the Heart, but Father Helstrom and Brother Dolus had been. 

\par \tab I\rquote d hoped to pray in that Sacristy once I was ordained. But by then, it was gone.
\par \tab The Heart of Faith was an iron tablet, circular and hardly larger than most dinner plates, cast f
rom the melted remains of the spear that killed Canus himself, wielded by a pagan warrior in the First Holy War. After his death, the spear had passed between the tribes of the savage Kadrush, a proud testament to their viciousness and revulsion for the T
rue Faith. Even to this day, they had not grown closer to God, by their own design. They remained a country of barbarians, faithful only to their false pagan gods and feral spirits.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi720\li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid6190639 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639 
Our Church had reclaimed the spear one thousand and thirty-one years ago, (
the history of the relic had become a subject of fascination and study for me since its theft) and in an attempt to cleanse the weapon of its sin and remake it into an object of worship that would bring hope, instead of anger and war, it was melted down a
nd re-forged into the Heart of Faith. 
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid6190639 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639 \tab 
The inscribed iron tablet was written in Ancient Amuraic, in a cuneiform alphabet very few monks were literate in, in this age. But the illustrations and etchings I had of it included a translation; a short Psalm written by the monk who\rquote 
d inscribed it, and the Seven Edicts of The Faith. 
\par \tab I would often recite the Seven Edicts to myself in Ancient Amuraic, when it suited me or when it was particularly appropriate to remind myself of them. I may not have been able to rea
d the cuneiform, but I knew some of the old tongue, especially prayers I\rquote d committed to my heart.
\par \tab Now was one such time.
\par \tab The Fifth Edict of the Faith was \lquote Presa lacriste bona maedal verducrisolum\rquote . \lquote The wolf shall not covet what is beyond his due\rquote . The meaning was simple- \lquote the wolf\rquote 
 referred to in every Edict was an ancient pronoun, a series of symbols in contention amongst scholars, in that it was likely meant to refer to all subjects of The Faith, not merely canines, let alone only wolves. But the Ancie
nts of a thousand years ago had less ways to refer to peoples aside from canines, at least in writing. So, all persons were \lquote wolves\rquote 
 in the Seven Edicts. At least, that is how I chose to interpret it, and also how most other learned men did. To do otherwise excluded all other races from the Edicts, and that was counter-productive.
\par \tab The meaning, as with most of the Edicts, could vary by interpretation. And that, too, was by design. The Edicts were meant to be called-upon in many stages of life, by many peoples, in more situations than merely the most obvious. To \lquote 
covet what is beyond his due\rquote  could mean not to desire that which one has not earned, or not been blessed with. Wealth, privilege, beauty, fame, etcetera. It was not forbidden to want and aspire for 
more, within the Faith. Aspiration had inspired betterment and ingenuity in men since time untold. But to want beyond your due, to say\'85
 resent the wealthy, the status of the Pedigree, or the God-gifted talent of another, was a destructive impulse that was injurious to the fabric of society, instilled no value for hard work and diligence, and often led to violent or covetous actions. 

\par \tab For instance, theft.
\par \tab And it would avail me little in my own trials, as well. Such as wishing for a more equitable situation
 than the discomfort I was currently experiencing, traveling in a slow-moving, lurching, reeking stagecoach.  Whispering holy scripture to myself, especially that which was relevant to my particular state of distress, was a means of keeping my mind center
ed.
\par \tab 
It probably gave no comfort to the six other passengers traveling in the coach with me, overhearing a large, imposing, armed man mumbling in repetition to himself in Ancient Amuraic. But- and I know this was uncharitable of me- some of their considerat
ion for my own well-being had been lacking, as well. So, I gave myself that minimal allowance.
\par \tab A heavyset, large terrier- a traveling salesman by the look of his briefcase- had been puffing on his pipe near feverishly the entire trip. Honestly, I wasn\rquote t sure how he hadn\rquote t made himself sick on the amount of tobacco he\rquote 
d been forcing all of us to inhale for the last ten hours. It had most likely contributed to the troubles of the other small mouse fellow beside him, who had to beg a sudden stop three times already today, to be sick on the side of the road. 
\par \tab And a fourth time, when he had not quite made it.
\par \tab Across from the three of us sat a family of four. Mixed-breed retrievers, I think, but it hardly mattered. The wife was frail and thin with a noticeable 
baby paunch still, trying in vain to keep her youngest, a newborn pup by the look of the both of them, from shrilly whining on }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f187\fs32\insrsid6190639\charrsid6912744 every}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f187\fs32\insrsid6190639  bump. God Bless her other child, a little girl barely three or four by the look of her, for helping her mother to try to keep the newborn calm. The father was of }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f187\fs32\insrsid6190639\charrsid6912744 no}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639 
 help, hissing and snapping at the poor woman as though there was anything more she could do to control her baby. They looked to be a family in trade who had fallen on hard times. They had two chests loaded in the back, likely all they owned. I couldn
\rquote t say what troubles had befallen them, but I knew I\rquote d be praying for them later that evening.
\par \tab Most of them, anyway.
\par \tab I had an excellent seat on a horse, and would infinitely prefer traveling in saddle over coach travel, regardless of the coming winter. Unfortunately, it just wasn\rquote 
t feasible for me to afford feeding a horse or pony on the small stipend I received from the Order while I pursued this investigation. I was fortunate enough to have been given the honor of this assignment at }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f187\fs32\insrsid6190639\charrsid11296731 all}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639 , and I knew that was on no merit of my own, but because I was in the intermittent period between my Ordination and the rigorous training I
\rquote d undergo once I was accepted by the Ministry of Inquisition, and the Order currently had little other use for me. Father Helstrom seemed to think I\rquote d be accepted by the Ministry if I applied now\'85 I was less certain.
\par \tab This was a rare time of free study in my career w
ithin my Order, and I intended to spend it pursuing the case that had lit the fire inside me to become an Inquisitor in the first place, whether my investigation bore fruit or not. There were, of course, many other more Senior Templar pursuing the Heart T
hief across Amuresca, or so Brother Dolus had told me. And I hardly thought myself above them.
\par \tab But I had a }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f187\fs32\insrsid6190639\charrsid12481258 lead}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639 . 
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi720\li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid6190639 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639 
A lead I had repeated up the chain, numerous times now over the last month, to no avail. I\rquote d taken to the lake country after barely a few days home in the Abbey, and had been following sightings ever since. The last town I\rquote 
d stayed in had been a place called Gloury, and was little more than a carriage house, a Church and a few farmsteads. But there were }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f187\fs32\insrsid6190639\charrsid3544256 five}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 
\ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639  sightings of her there that I\rquote d been able to confirm, from all of the staff at the carriage house. She- or someone who bore a startling resemblance to her- had stayed the night. She\rquote 
d been wearing her male disguise in order to travel alone, but I knew better than to let that deter me. I\rquote d even asked to stay in the same room, to see if I could catch her scent remaining there. 
\par No luck, there. They had already cleaned the linens, and while the room still had the inklings of a feline that tickled at the back of my nose, she\rquote d either masked
 her scent, changed her scent enough to be unrecognizable, or I was on the trail of the wrong feline.
\par But that couldn\rquote t be it. I was more certain now than I\rquote d ever been. The Equestrian Show circuit was some kind of key in how she moved through the lake cou
ntry, I was sure of it. There was too much overlap to be otherwise. Between the accounts of her I\rquote 
d found by paws-on-the-ground canvasing, to a string of suspicious carriage lockbox thefts, culminating in a burglary at a private art showing at a Pedigree\rquote s
 country estate, it all seemed to orbit around the Show Circuit. Young Lords had little else to do in the lake country in late autumn than show off their Dressage skills, after all. 
\par It made sense. The sport competitions meant small gatherings of elites in
 relatively rural locations, where house guards would be minimal. A guaranteed route to follow if one were in the market to rob the idle rich, and slip into their confidence at gathers where hangers-on that were unknown previously to the estate would not 
raise eyebrows. The Heart Thief could conceal themselves as a lady\rquote s maid, a coachman, even a groom for the horses.
\par And so, upon traveling said circuit, I had eventually caught her trail. Or so it seemed. A slender cat with tall ears, black-furred or spotte
d intermittently, although I had to attest the greater number of spotted sightings to the obvious- such a pelt being more likely to stick out in peoples\rquote 
 memory. Smartly-dressed or at least neat and tidy, when she deigned to pretend at being lowlier. A low
 profile, but quiet and polite to the few who remembered sharing a conversation with her. She was not overly gregarious, never brazen or rude, but always went leaving a decent impression of herself in the minds of those who\rquote d seen her. 
\par She was a dove. Commonplace, easy to ignore, but pleasing to take note of. Even one of the Pedigree ladies whose trunk had been burglarized remembered her fondly, never the wiser that it was the demure feline at market whom they\rquote 
d bought apples from who had pilfered their purse and key. I don\rquote t think she\rquote d put the pieces together even after I\rquote d spoken to her. She only mentioned remembering the feline because I\rquote 
d asked, and then wondered aloud if she may be someone to question\'85 someone who may have seen the ruffian who\rquote d pick-pocketed her. She was convinced it was one of a group of young rat lads she seemed to remember as suspicious.  
\par I did not want to believe that I\rquote d fooled myself, that I was, as Father Helstrom warned against, chasing ghosts. My every suspicion confirmed I 
was on the right path, and while I would never be so bold as to claim He was directly guiding me, I felt an affirmation in my soul every time I heard a new account of her. It made something in my chest lift like my body was tumbling, my breath coming shor
ter, faster\'85 I just }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f187\fs32\insrsid6190639\charrsid13704315 knew}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639  I was growing closer in this chase\'85
 could all but feel my quarry, the distance between us shortening\'85
\par If no one else in the Order thought what I\rquote d found was compelling, so be it. I would do this alone. Father Helstrom\rquote s concerned an
d obviously irritated letters were coming rapid-fire these days, attempting to recall me home. He was nearly my only contact with the Order that was consistent though, other than my check-ins with a few Parish Priests and one House Templar at the country 
estate that had been burglarized. And he seemed dubious that the criminal had been who I claimed she was.
\par A sudden yelp snapped me out of my reverie, and I thought at first it had been the pup, but it sounded too mature for that. I flicked my eyes open and glanced up from where my rosary was clutched in my left paw. 
\par The wife was shying away from her husband, and I didn\rquote t miss the way his hand was extended briefly before retreating back into the folds of his coat. I hadn\rquote t seen whatever had happened, but the body language was clear.
\par The terrier blew out another long puff from his pipe, and the mouse continued to look sick. Neither remarked on whatever had occurred between the married couple.
\par Neither did I. But I stopped praying, and started counting the miles instead. By the road markers, we were close to our destination. A sizeable town called Wrenwarren.
\par I was unsettled. Uncomfortable. Frustrated. And wound too tight, from the weeks of being on the chase. I\rquote d also been having trouble sleeping on the road, in unfamiliar spaces and around a constantly-shifting daily schedule. As well as\'85
 other persistent nighttime troubles. 
\par But that was no excuse.
\par It felt like we\rquote d slowed down to a crawl for that last stretch, but at length, finally\'85 salvation. The coach came 
to a clattering, jerking stop- and then another start- and then another, final, stop. It was dusk light outside, the flickering lanterns adorning the front of the hectic-sounding carriage house we\rquote 
d pulled into casting a smeared glare across the windows. I had gotten nothing but a blurred look at the town, when the doors finally swung open and fresh air, light, and freedom beckoned me out.
\par Fresh-ish, anyway. The first overwhelming scent was fresh\'85 manure, charred cooking and the ever-present undercurrent of
 soot from chimneys. And the wind that buffeted the door to nearly clatter closed again was bitterly cold, far more so than I remembered any night prior on this trip so far. Winter was making an early arrival this year.
\par Tucking my cloak to the side, I let the mouse hurry past me, nearly stumbling over my legs in his haste to get outside. I wasn\rquote t certain if he was just eager to be out, or about to be sick again, and I wasn\rquote t keen on finding out.
\par \'93Little vermin didn\rquote t make this trip any more pleasant for the rest of us, now did he?\'94 the terrier huffed from behind me, his bulk making the seat creak as he stowed his pipe, finally.
\par I gave him a look that I\rquote m certain did not conceal my incredulity, before murmuring a quiet but audible, \'93Thy sins are a projection, as stained-glass colors the face of the scapegoat.\'94
\par \'93More bloody choir nonsense\'85 what are you on about?\'94 the man asked distractedly as he struggled into his outer coat. \'93Move then, would you? You simple?\'94
\par I turned and shouldered through the door, finding 
my footing on the step-down, and ignoring any more grumblings from the man. The coachman and his auxiliary driver were already unstrapping luggage from the back of the box, so after a quick moment spent soaking in the luxury of being able to stretch my le
gs, I moved in that direction to collect my few belongings.
\par The one travel bag I carried was a leather saddle bag with versatile straps that could be let in, adjusted}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid13777146 ,}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639  and converted to a }
{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid13777146 traveler\rquote s}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639  pack. The shoulder straps hooked directly into the Templar harness}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f187\fs32\insrsid13777146 ,}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639 
 which was something of a mark of office for us. We all wore variations of it, most carried a weapon or two on it as well, but primarily, it was for this. We were outfitted to travel at a moments\rquote  notice if need-be, and our vestments were a testame
nt to practicality, honed by centuries of missionary work }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid13777146 back when that was the norm, }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639 
before the idea of House Templar had been formalized. }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid13777146 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639 Templar were Crusaders, now and for all time. We were meant to travel and bring the Word.
\par Of course, Templar of old would have done so on horseback. Not a box coach.
\par Adjusting the pack took a matter of minutes; I\rquote d been doing it since I was ten, so I was rather proficient at it now. Still, it delayed me long enough that I unfortunately witnessed what happened next.
\par The tradesman\rquote s family had two large, and by the look of them }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f187\fs32\insrsid6190639\charrsid8528646 heavy}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639  trunks, slicked with cold rain fr
om hours ago and stacked beside one another on the back of the box. Once they were unstrapped, the auxiliary coachman left, leaving the exhausted couple to unload the trunks themselves. 
\par The husband managed with some obvious strain to heft the one, his sho
ulder sagging with the weight, but the wife seemed more hesitant. I watched her shift her swaddled pup carefully against her chest and shoulder with one hand, while she began to ease the trunk by the handle towards the edge of the platform to pull it down
.
\par That was about all I could take.
\par Concern overriding my reason, I strode forward and extended a hand to the woman to gesture at the trunk, raising my voice over the background din from the busy evening street to say, \'93Pray, let me help you with that-\'93
\par I must have startled her, and with good reason. }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid13777146 I had}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639 
 spoken too loud and too suddenly, and from behind her no less. Her shoulders tensed up, she sucked in a shrill breath and stumbled back a pace, surprise or panic compelling her to shy away from me as she had from her husband, earlier.
\par In the process, she let go of her grip on the trunk, and unbalanced\'85 it fell. 
\par Thankfully}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid13777146 ,}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639  it didn\rquote t fall on her feet, or anyone else\rquote 
s. But it thudded down onto the wet, muddy street and tumbled onto its back, whatever was inside it no doubt being tossed a}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid13777146 round}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639 
 inside. I didn\rquote t hear any noise that suggested it held anything fragile, but it }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f187\fs32\insrsid6190639\charrsid9005382 could}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639 
