t occur to me until much later. Although by the law, he could handle the disciplining of his family in the manner of his choosing, there were }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f187\fs32\insrsid6190639\charrsid13456226 limits}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639 .}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f187\fs32\insrsid6190639\charrsid13456226  }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639 
\par \'93Leo, please,\'94 the woman begged, tugging away from }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid13777146 me,}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639  and scrambling to stand again. I, of course, released her. \'93
The trunk is fine, it\rquote s just a bit of mud}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid13777146 - \'93}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639 
\par \'93I\rquote m not talking to you, slag!\'94 the small man snarled past his fangs. He went to shove her and roar something else in my face, but the woman tried to grab at his arm to pull him away, and he easily sho
ok her off. This time, I had no chance to catch her.
\par The pup was still held steadfast in her one arm, }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid13777146 but she lost her balance. She fell, mostly on her hip,}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639 
 splayed to her side in the mud now, beside the trunk. The man was shouting something again, but there was too much blood pounding in my ears. My body was hot\'85 my extremities absolutely lit up with the desire to }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f187\fs32\insrsid6190639\charrsid5592567 move them}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639 .
\par If there is a way to contain these fires when they rage in me, I have not yet found it.
\par I moved in front of her as he stepped towards her. He may have simply been wanting to pull her to her feet; I\rquote d never know. He found his path blocked, he stomped forward despite it, and swung out an arm. I think }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 
\ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid13777146 he had}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639  been trying to shove me aside, as he had his wife.
\par It was enough.
\par I was no skinny young woman clutching an infant to my breast. I raised my own arm concisely in between his bicep and chest, tipped him off-balance with a slight press, and swung my other fist up in an arc to belt him in the muzzle.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid13777146 I had}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639  meant to stun him, but I was less 
than precise, and I probably put more into it than I should have. The day had frayed me around the edges, and it would be dishonest not to admit some part of me wanted to hurt this particular man.
\par At least three people audibly gasped or }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid13777146 shouted}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639  in alarm on
 the street beside us, and I swear I could feel the eyes of people watching us from their second-story vantage point above in the carriage house. 
\par The man crumbled to the ground clutching at his muzzle, a splatter of spittle and blood beside him on the pav
ing stone. I flexed my fingers, and huffed out a puff of steam into the frigid air, trying to calm myself. I held back from doing any more, and didn\rquote t bother with words for the man. I would not have been eloquent, had I tried.
\par His wife }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid13777146 moaned worriedly}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639  and
 went to her knees beside him, hunkering close and turning towards me with unshed tears in her eyes. \'93}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid13777146 Just l}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639 eave us }{
\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f187\fs32\insrsid6190639\charrsid5787888 be}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f187\fs32\insrsid13777146 , please}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639 !\'94
\par I blinked at her, stunned and finding myself gesturing uselessly at the man. \'93He-\'93 
\par \'93}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f187\fs32\insrsid6190639\charrsid13777146 He}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid13777146  is}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639  my husband!\'94
 she cried- truly cried, she was sobbing now- at me. \'93My children\rquote s father!\'94
\par \'93I\rquote m sorry, ma\rquote am,\'94 I found myself saying, licking at my cold, dry muzzle. \'93Are you-\'93
\par \'93I asked you to leave us be!\'94 she said again, vehemently, petting back her husband\rquote s ears. He was coming to, he hadn\rquote t been unconscious only stunned, and while he didn\rquote t look gravely injured, he wasn\rquote 
t handling the blow to the nose as well as his wife had.
\par Well. She likely had more experience.
\par \'93We want nothing from the Church,\'94 she said, trembling. \'93What good are you?\'94
\par I gathered my cloak around myself, willing my body to move through the rigidity of my suddenly cooling blood. \'93Blessings on you and your family,\'94 I murmured out, quickly. 
\par \'93What good are your blessings?!\'94 she demanded of my back, as I retreated.
\par Shutters clattered overhead, signaling even the onlookers had beaten a hasty retreat, the small crowd that had gathered parting as I moved through them. All but the coachman, an o
ld, greying badger who seemed oddly unaffected by the happenings. He stood beside the larger of the two dappled horses that been pulling the coach, calling to me, \'93He swung first, lad. I\rquote 
ll tell the peace officers that if they come asking around.\'94
\par \'93They won\rquote t,\'94 I said quietly, making my way towards the carriage house door. The coachman was either essentially lying or hadn\rquote 
t gotten a good look; you could claim many things about what had happened, but it would be a stretch to say he\rquote d swung intending to strike me. Either way, the badger was telling me he\rquote d bear false witness. Which didn\rquote t comfort me.}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639\charrsid13456226 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639 It wouldn\rquote t matter. The man I\rquote d struck would never bring this up with the town guard. }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid9860309 He had}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639  sat with me long enough in that box to have something of an idea who and what I was. And no one in his current situation would have the means or wherewithal to bring charges against a holy man.
\par The knowledge that I would suffer no punishment whatsoever for my moment of anger, coupled with the intense relief I felt at having expressed it, and a selfish inner voice that sung, practically jeered \lquote retribution!\rquote 
, had my stomach curdling. I was so worn-down by the ordeals of the day that I temporarily forgot my quarry almost entirely, foregoing my usual ritual in each new town of questioning the carriage house staff first. I\rquote 
d tried to make a habit of it, since discovering whether or not the feline had stayed at one of the few places in town open to non-canine travelers was the most important question to address, first thing. If she\rquote 
d been through very recently, it meant I was close.  
\par I was in no hurry to get back on the road, so there was probably a part of me that hoped I\rquote d chosen the wrong town again. Her pattern of movement was akin to the veins on a leaf, darting sporadically 
from place to place, but always inevitably following the central vein- the Eastern Post Road. If not here, I\rquote d catch her trail again soon.
\par And I was exhausted. I climbed the stairs to my little room on the second story, and shed my vestments and accoutrements like they were thrice as heavy as they were. As I folded and stowed them in the room\rquote 
s small footlocker, I briefly considered whether or not it would be worth it to dress in my laundered clothing to go down for dinner\'85 and just as quickly dismissed the
 thought. I was too tired. My throat was scratchy from what I feared might be a cold coming on- Brother Dolus was quite right there, I was dreadfully prone to them- and the scuffle outside had spoiled whatever remaining appetite I may have had.
\par I dug my rosary up off the end table where I\rquote d lain it down when I was dressing, unwrapped the coils of it and draped it around my neck, and stiffly settled down on my knees to begin my evening prayers.
\par I ended up praying for every soul who\rquote d shared that carriage ride with me today. Even the husband. Lord knew what troubles that family had faced. They needed help. God willing, they\rquote d get it.
\par 
\par 
\par That night, I found myself not in the crumbling, unfamiliar church that had been the meeting }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid3873516 spot}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639  for many of my confusing}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid4087786  nightly}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639  interludes with the Heart Thief\'85
 a far-too-frequent denizen in my dreamscapes of late. But instead, a wistful, insubstantial place. I was indoors, that much at least I could tell. There was a window, and through it I caught glimpses of the moon.
\par W
aning, Waxing, Full, all three stood in stark relief in the sky. At the same time. The moonlight painted the contours of the room in violet twilight, white near the edges where the twisting folds of sheets hugged tightly to the bodies entwined beneath the
m.
\par My figure, my body, I realized, was outlined beneath. Of course it was. I was in bed\'85 had been in bed. I was sleeping. Wasn\rquote t I?
\par Except this wasn\rquote t my tiny room at the carriage house, with the sloping ceiling too short for me that I\rquote d had to duck under earlier that night. Nor was it the single bed too s}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid3873516 
mall}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639  for my frame, that I\rquote 
d knelt beside to pray. In fact, the size of the space I lay in seemed to shift to accommodate, go on until it touched the walls. The entire room, the entire space, molded around us, the sheets had no corners, the bed no dips or edges.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid3873516 And }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639 I wasn\rquote t alone.
\par Panic gripped me and sunk its claws in, and despite the inherent lethargy and seeming peace of the scene I found myself in, that panic did not recede with time. It grew, blooming inside me}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid3873516 , }{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639 
a rose whose vines were wound tight around my heart. Half-remembered scenes played out on the walls, fuzzy around the edges like the block-printed ink illustrations inside the fashion magazines I\rquote d studied.
\par I saw her in those }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid3873516 etchings as though they were projected on the walls}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639 
, wearing the outfit that had led me here, to these counties. The ink lines traced the contours of her cheeks, her muzzle, her ears. They clotted at the tips of long lashes as she turned to regard me, in that crumbling church
. Some of the memories were real, I vaguely thought, plucked from our time spent in the dove garden. Some of them were probably from other dreams.
\par Dreams. I was dreaming. This wasn\rquote t real.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid3873516  So that meant\'85}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639  
\par The figure that lay obscured beneath the sheets, curled and clutched around me so tightly. It was}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid3873516  probably}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639  her. It had to be her.

\par It was }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f187\fs32\insrsid6190639\charrsid1257375 always}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639  her, these days. 
\par I could not will myself to wake, and lying there staring down the expanse of our sheet-wrapped forms, unable to move and knowing, }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f187\fs32\insrsid6190639\charrsid1257375 feeling}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 
\ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639  her there, certain it was her\'85
 it was agony. A month now, I had spent doggedly tailing her between hamlets and tiny, unfamiliar cities, a month now on the road, a stranger everywhere I went, alone with my obsessive thoughts, pursued by my Father\rquote s anxious letters
, all to find her }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f187\fs32\insrsid6190639\charrsid16086308 and now she was right here}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639 .
\par It was wish-fulfillment, I knew. I could always find her in my dreams if not in the waking world, but why God.
\par Why, in this way?
\par Why was she 
warm in my arms, her fur tufted between my fingertips, her breath on my collarbone, coming out in steady, slumbering puffs? Her muzzle tucked just enough beneath the covers that I would need to pull them back to see her. To confirm what I already knew.

\par She was nude. We both were. I didn\rquote 
t need to push off the clinging sheets to know it, I could see it in the jut of her hip where one slender leg was wrapped about my thigh. I could tell by the feel of her arm, looped around my chest, petite fingers splayed ac
ross my ribs. I could feel the subtle prick of her claws, extending for a moment as she twitched in her sleep.
\par She smelled like the honeysuckle that always inevitably overgrew in the orchard each year. Like spring. These dreams, her body wrapped around mine now\'85 }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid3873516 
they were as inexorable as the changing seasons}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639 
. Bursting through every crack and open window, clinging to every surface like the fragrant pollen that would dust the covers of the books in the library and find its way into your fur and cling there.
\par She was }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f187\fs32\insrsid6190639\charrsid9385008 right there}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639 
. All I needed to do was pull back the sheets. Here, at least, my chase was over. Even if it was all wrong, even if }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f187\fs32\insrsid6190639\charrsid9385008 this}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f187\fs32\insrsid6190639  was wrong, could I not experience that moment of exultation? Here, I had caught her.
\par Here, she was mine.
\par 
\par I awoke with a sucked-in gasp, and the very first thing I did was fling back the tangled sheets twisted around my lower half and legs. I sat upright, panting and blinking blearily in the dark, the dream\rquote 
s edges at last receding, dissolving into the waking world. The
 lavender faded into dim orange and black, what little light illuminated the cracking paint and sloping edges of the small room coming from the narrow, clouded window that faced a street lamp outside. 
\par It was still night. I knew not when, but\'85 full dark. 
\par Bracing myself, my eyes traveled down my body, past the steady rise and fall of my furred chest to my legs and the balled-up crumple of sheets and a threadbare comforter. The stove pipe that ran through this room- its only source of heat- had gone cold wh
en the kitchens below went dormant for the night. The room was frigid. 
\par But my body felt hot. Stiflingly warm, in fact, so much so that I could not stop panting. 
\par I groaned out loud in frustration when I glanced at the state of my smallclothes. I had so few opportunities to launder my garments on the road, so I was glad at least that I hadn\rquote t soiled them again\'85
\par \'85 but the state I found myself in was\'85 obscene. And unfortunately, a common occurrence these days. All but guaranteed when I had these dreams too, which regardless of Brother Dolus\rquote s}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f187\fs32\insrsid3873516  assurances}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639 , made me terribly anxious to reflect upon.
\par It was a maddeningly-stubborn condition, and beyond being horribly injurious to my decency should I need to }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f187\fs32\insrsid6190639\charrsid3555299 go}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f187\fs32\insrsid6190639  anywhere, it made me feel great physical discomfort the whole of the while I was forced to endure it. At least on the rare occasions I spilled my seed in my sleep, the issue went dormant swiftly, afterwards.
\par When it was like this, I was often\'85 to put it bluntly, }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f187\fs32\insrsid6190639\charrsid3555299 stuck}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639  with it. For some time. And there seemed to 
be no rhyme or reason to when it would give up the ghost. Nor any remedy for the condition that worked reliably. Cold water splashed in my face might help, and morning exercises could chase it away. Prayer had proved useful once or twice. But nothing work
ed every time. Not even all three.
\par I swung my legs over the side of the small bed, scrubbing my palms back and forth up over my muzzle and forehead. I considered and then dismissed several verses I might try, but my mind was still tangled up in the dream, a
nd recitation of anything coherent may have been beyond me. I mumbled the opening verse of Saint Gerard\rquote s Prayer for Forgiveness once, twice, the remnant dying on my tongue before I could really get underway.
\par I didn\rquote t deserve forgiveness. 
\par \'93Oh Lord,\'94 I mumbled into my hands, \'93what is wrong with me? Please deliver your servant some clarity. Guide me, I beg of you.\'94
\par Something flickered across my eyeline. Rubbing my eyes again and letting my paws fall into my lap, I squinted across the room. It was barely larger than a closet, so there wasn\rquote 
t much to see. My bag, the hook on the wall my harness and weapons were hung from, the footlocker my clothing was stored in, the door, and the strips of dim light coming from underneath it. There was a lantern in the hallway
 right outside my room, lighting the way downstairs to the privy for all of the upstairs guests.
\par Why was the line broken?
\par I blinked, trying to be certain I was seeing correctly. But, yes. The thin line of light beneath my door was broken in two places, about evenly-spaced apart. My memory from the night before hadn\rquote 
t been great, but I recalled taking note of the lantern light because it was so bright, I\rquote d worried the illumination coming in under the door might make it difficult to sleep. I slept better in full darkness.
\par Someone was standing outside my door.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f187\fs32\insrsid6190639\charrsid11622589 Right}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639 
 outside my door. Needlessly close, in fact, unless they were about to knock, or were pressed against it for some reason-
\par I sprung to my feet so fast, I forgot how low the sloping ceiling was. My sku
ll collided with one of the wooden beams overhead, a pained bark leaving my muzzle before I could entirely quash it. Sparks danced behind my eyes and I shook my head a few times, getting my bearings.
\par The shadow behind the door shifted and changed, and then I heard it. Not footsteps beating a hasty retreat, as I imagined I\rquote d hear after making that much noise. No.
\par Quiet, muffled laughter. Almost whispered, breathy\'85 and brief.
\par Then footsteps, although hardly in a rush. Soft paws, padding off down the hallway. To the left.
\par  I clicked my jaw shut, shook the daze off one last time, felt my drill training kick in and strode across the room purposefully, eschewing my vestments save the pair of trousers I\rquote d left hanging on one of the coat hooks in the room, to dry of
f the damp. I was shouldering into my harness before I\rquote d so much as finished buttoning up. I could don my harness in less than half a minute when I was focused, and I think I got it done in twenty seconds this time, including affixing my long knife
\rquote s scabbard. I threw my surcoat on over my shoulders as I unlocked and opened the door, moving out into the hallway while still pulling my arms through the sleeves. I didn\rquote t bother closing it\'85 the harness was meant to go on }{\rtlch\fcs1 
\ai\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f187\fs32\insrsid6190639\charrsid15207107 over}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639  it anyway. But if I was about to go outside for any reason, I needed to have some measure of decency.
\par There was no time to really consider my state of disarray, not with the reality of someone }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f187\fs32\insrsid6190639\charrsid15207107 spying}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639 
 on me being all but confirmed. Was it something to do with the altercation in the street earlier? Something benign, like the children of the family who ran the carriage house, sneaking about at night?
\par Frightful late for that.
\par I went left down the hallway, which was not the direction to the staircase, since my room was closest to that. It was the cheapest\'85 being the loudest and so close to the lantern, so of course I\rquote 
d taken it. The hallway to the left led to nine other rooms, some small like mine, some presumably larger b
ased on the spacing. There was one other lantern down at the distant end of the garishly wallpaper-ed walls- peonies, in colors that even I could tell did }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f187\fs32\insrsid6190639\charrsid10760522 not}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639  work well together- and a weathered, faded carpet that only barely masked the squeak of the floorboards beneath it, as I slowly made my way down.
\par The hallway was dimly-lit, but I still would have seen someone if there were anyone there to see. Had it taken me long enough to arm and dress myself that the person had somehow come back, and made their way to the staircase?
\par No. I would have heard that. I\rquote d had my eyes on the light under the door the whole time, and the stairs were, as aforementioned, loud. Whoever it was, they\rquote d come back this way and gone into their room.
\par For just a moment, I felt like a fool. Ungodly hour or no, it must have been a child, playing in the hall. Or a peeping adult, I supposed. Why had I been so certain this was dangerous, or some
thing worrisome enough that I needed a weapon on me? If I ran into someone in this hallway right now, obviously armed and with my surcoat gaping open to my bare chest, in the middle of the night, they would justly think I was a madman.
\par At the very least, my\'85 \lquote issue\rquote  from earlier had receded. The pain from striking my head on the ceiling had seen to that. Solution found, I suppose.
\par Something occurred to me as I drew closer to the end of the hall. One of the doors, the one to the left at the very end, was ajar. By at least a few inches. Warm light pooled out onto the dusty carpet, and a strange scent grew stronger the closer I got.

\par Some kind of\'85 ink? Dye? I\rquote d smelled something like it before, I was certain of it\'85
\par I gasped aloud, then clenched my teeth shut, and took the last few strides towards the end of the hallway quickly, while trying to be as quiet as I could. If I looked like a foolish madman, barging into someone\rquote 
s room in the middle of the night, so be it. I was a bloody }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f187\fs32\insrsid6190639\charrsid4095943 Templar}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \f187\fs32\insrsid6190639  on a mission, I\rquote d make m
y apologies later if need be. But if I was right, and I turned heel now to fully dress or get my other things, whoever was }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f187\fs32\insrsid6190639\charrsid4095943 in}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f187\fs32\insrsid6190639  there would have a chance to escape. And that was a chance I simply was not willing to take.
\par I stopped only when I reached and boxed in the doorway, pressing my side to the doorframe, and leaning slowly to tip my head inside.
